the final push to rescue the last of the  13 soccer players and their coach  trapped in a Thai cave is almost over at  least three more boys have been pulled  out so far this morning as we've  reported divers guided eight of the boys  to safety in the first two days of the  rescue effort  mark Strassmann went inside a similar  cave system in Florida to show us some  of the conditions the soccer team  endured for more than two weeks mark  filed this report from Marianna Florida  good morning I'm standing about fifty  feet underground in the only developed  cave for touring in the entire state of  Florida wild caves are much more  challenging in fact this space is about  as soft and family friendly as caving  gets but even here you do get a sense of  how someone could get into trouble in a  hurry this is the deepest point of the  cavern here here we're about 60 feet  below the surface park ranger billy  bailey took us into this half mile long  limestone cave and if you had scuba gear  could you go even further  sure temperature in here is what 65  degrees no matter what team is you  outside it's always about 65 degrees  here in the cave with all its twists and  turns parts of this 38 million year old  cave are just above the water table  during a heavy rain the water inside has  risen as much as six feet you wouldn't  be able to go into this part of the  Kaveri when it was flooded when that  happens the Park Rangers here get plenty  of warnings not so in Thailand another  difference this cave has been developed  the walkways are level railings and  lights are everywhere in a wild cave  you'd be spelunking or cave exploring is  crawling so you would need to knee pads  elbow pads helmet several sources of  light  Bailey showed us what it's like during a  caves natural state total darkness Wow  you know she cannot see your hand in  front of your face it's disorienting  really fast it also makes it impossible  to see all the stalactites and  stalagmites those limestone rock  formations jutting up from the ceiling  and floor in the dark  all this stuff can hurt sure if you hit  your head incidentally hers and when you  look at what's happening in Thailand  what goes  he's being scared not knowing what  tomorrow would bring it's just a feeling  of helplessness Billy told us that no  one has ever been trapped in here and  visitors are never more than five  minutes from an exit but remember in  Thailand that soccer team was trapped  more than two miles from daylight John  thank you
 

